1
00:00:10,972 --> 00:00:11,432
[SPEAKER_00]: What were you saying?

2
00:00:11,472 --> 00:00:12,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Any more realms of the God realm?

3
00:00:12,853 --> 00:00:19,317
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the whole... the words... you know, brahmacharya is the way of... the holy life.

4
00:00:19,437 --> 00:00:19,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

5
00:00:19,837 --> 00:00:20,858
[SPEAKER_02]: The way of the gods.

6
00:00:20,878 --> 00:00:22,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the way of the gods, yeah.

7
00:00:22,359 --> 00:00:23,360
[SPEAKER_00]: The way of the brahmas.

8
00:00:24,140 --> 00:00:30,384
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the more... you know... Yeah, the cleaner, the more virtues, the loftier...

9
00:00:31,304 --> 00:00:32,525
[SPEAKER_00]: The more God-life.

10
00:00:32,565 --> 00:00:34,106
[SPEAKER_00]: The human life is lead.

11
00:00:34,967 --> 00:00:36,308
[SPEAKER_00]: It becomes more God-life.

12
00:00:36,728 --> 00:00:36,988
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

13
00:00:38,622 --> 00:00:46,464
[SPEAKER_00]: The more proliferated, the more, based on senses, revolving around senses, absorbed in the senses, the more animal-like it is.

14
00:00:48,524 --> 00:00:52,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Based around sensual pleasures and gratification and so on, it's more like animals.

15
00:00:53,825 --> 00:01:05,428
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the human choice, based on which you perform certain actions, awesome or not, kind of determines your future direction.

16
00:01:11,262 --> 00:01:16,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's a sort of realm in between that gives you a glimpse of both.

17
00:01:17,567 --> 00:01:21,451
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, animals don't have much... they're fully responsible, but they don't have much choice.

18
00:01:22,852 --> 00:01:24,713
[SPEAKER_00]: And they suddenly can't behave like gods.

19
00:01:26,315 --> 00:01:32,280
[SPEAKER_00]: And they can sometimes exhibit human-like behavior, which is already a big step forward for an animal.

20
00:01:33,045 --> 00:01:33,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, wow, look at that.

21
00:01:35,166 --> 00:01:38,486
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, for gods, they can't behave.

22
00:01:39,067 --> 00:01:40,887
[SPEAKER_00]: It's harder for them to behave like animals.

23
00:01:42,448 --> 00:01:56,711
[SPEAKER_00]: But for humans, being kind of in between, we can get both, which is why the distinction is clear, which is why the human realm has both suffering and pleasure to such an extent that it's most optimal for practice.

24
00:01:57,051 --> 00:01:58,732
[SPEAKER_00]: We get both, we can really recognize.

25
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:02,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Animals are just so deeply in pain, basically.

26
00:02:03,122 --> 00:02:05,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Constant drive, constant fear, constant need.

27
00:02:07,145 --> 00:02:09,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Not much reflection, so they don't really know anything better.

28
00:02:10,347 --> 00:02:13,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Dogs, on the other hand, they're usually intoxicated with pleasure.

29
00:02:13,850 --> 00:02:18,053
[SPEAKER_00]: They almost never experience pain, so they don't stop and think about it.

30
00:02:19,121 --> 00:02:28,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Human realm is exposed to both, and you kind of have the threat of the pain, of the suffering, but then you do have pleasure and gratification, which is also valid, valid phenomenon.

31
00:02:29,347 --> 00:02:33,010
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all, that's like the most optimal, to really develop wisdom.

32
00:02:36,832 --> 00:02:40,775
[SPEAKER_02]: It's really, yeah, it's really effective on the... like just the animals.

33
00:02:41,868 --> 00:02:43,289
[SPEAKER_02]: Just to watch a dog, you know?

34
00:02:43,869 --> 00:02:44,109
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

35
00:02:44,489 --> 00:02:46,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Just to see how agitated it is.

36
00:02:47,051 --> 00:02:50,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, like, you know, when you train, when you train a dog...

37
00:02:51,527 --> 00:02:54,950
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to get it holistic, we have to get his attention.

38
00:02:56,010 --> 00:03:03,316
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, through a little repetition in training, you keep learning how to get his attention, and that's when you tell him, well, come on, and he always listens.

39
00:03:03,916 --> 00:03:10,862
[SPEAKER_00]: But you realize how quickly that attention changes, and you know sometimes there's no point saying now, I don't have... like something else has taken.

40
00:03:11,322 --> 00:03:18,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, the well-trained dog would be able to be snagged out of whatever has taken his attention, no matter what.

41
00:03:19,989 --> 00:03:24,212
[SPEAKER_00]: But that requires, you know, obviously, it depends on the brain and the skill of a trainer and so on.

42
00:03:24,552 --> 00:03:25,333
[SPEAKER_00]: But it all revolves.

43
00:03:25,633 --> 00:03:29,797
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you do that, you notice how that attention is just all over the place, constant.

44
00:03:30,457 --> 00:03:36,923
[SPEAKER_00]: Sound, sound, smell, smell, touch, smell, smell, sound, sound, running, left, right, bite, ah, something, agitate, jump, calm, hungry.

45
00:03:37,543 --> 00:03:37,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Constant.

46
00:03:38,184 --> 00:03:38,704
[SPEAKER_00]: It's constant.

47
00:03:39,545 --> 00:03:40,185
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the enemy.

48
00:03:40,205 --> 00:03:41,947
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you look at the little, cute little bird.

49
00:03:42,627 --> 00:03:45,550
[SPEAKER_00]: You look at it, it's just constant terror, constant threats.

50
00:03:46,497 --> 00:03:48,339
[SPEAKER_00]: Just keeps looking around, just twitching.

51
00:03:48,419 --> 00:03:53,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Constantly twitching, because next moment, might be very last, there's some attack or something.

52
00:03:55,004 --> 00:03:59,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Either birds, other praying animals, or things of that nature.

53
00:03:59,488 --> 00:04:01,309
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just constantly... and that's the animal at all.

54
00:04:01,470 --> 00:04:05,593
[SPEAKER_00]: Constant struggle, constant fight, constant survival.

55
00:04:05,853 --> 00:04:13,580
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, yeah, well, the other day we were... for some people like to get into... like, you hear this whole

56
00:04:14,886 --> 00:04:19,650
[SPEAKER_02]: Getting into contact with the animal spirits and stuff like that, that whole... No, that's ridiculous.

57
00:04:19,670 --> 00:04:27,296
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, as if that the lion was kept, you know, the lion's got no fear or... You can use the simile.

58
00:04:27,356 --> 00:04:31,479
[SPEAKER_00]: You can say the lion is the fearless, you become fearless, but you don't end with it in life.

59
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:33,921
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you don't... Because what else is lion?

60
00:04:34,101 --> 00:04:36,203
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a killer, it's a murderer, it's a rapist.

61
00:04:37,084 --> 00:04:39,266
[SPEAKER_00]: If that's what lions are, male lions.

62
00:04:40,888 --> 00:04:50,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, when people glorify animals as if they kind of have some deeper sixth sense that we don't have... Yeah, they have their own ways of perception, sure.

63
00:04:51,030 --> 00:04:54,852
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, they can detect subtler things, better smells, but that's the thing.

64
00:04:54,932 --> 00:05:09,180
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not necessarily... it's not better, it's actually more proliferated, more particular, more based upon it, which means less reflection of power you have in regard to your senses, more you're pulled, driven, constant drive.

65
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:10,881
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what animals are like.

66
00:05:12,041 --> 00:05:13,402
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this whole kind of, you know,

67
00:05:14,295 --> 00:05:17,316
[SPEAKER_00]: One with the nature, like, I must not interfere with the nature self.

68
00:05:17,396 --> 00:05:21,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's... that's quite silly, actually, because nature is what saṃsāra is.

69
00:05:22,638 --> 00:05:24,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Nature wants you to stay ignorant.

70
00:05:25,278 --> 00:05:27,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Nature is ignorant and it's all about ignorance.

71
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:32,401
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not getting in touch with nature, becoming one with nature.

72
00:05:32,881 --> 00:05:40,183
[SPEAKER_00]: It means becoming one with your ignorance, fully endorsing it as a valid... as a valid thing that should be cherished and protected.

73
00:05:41,345 --> 00:05:42,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Because nature is what it is.

74
00:05:42,947 --> 00:05:44,989
[SPEAKER_00]: And what it is, it's affected with the region.

75
00:05:46,051 --> 00:05:49,255
[SPEAKER_00]: So, going against the stream means going against the nature.

76
00:05:50,112 --> 00:05:50,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure.

77
00:05:51,073 --> 00:05:54,995
[SPEAKER_00]: The nature of the body is to run after its, like, senses.

78
00:05:55,335 --> 00:05:56,516
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what the suttas say.

79
00:05:56,856 --> 00:05:59,938
[SPEAKER_00]: Senses, five senses, are like five wild animals.

80
00:05:59,958 --> 00:06:02,459
[SPEAKER_00]: They have their feeding grounds, and that's all they want to do.

81
00:06:02,519 --> 00:06:05,641
[SPEAKER_00]: They just want to go and feed in their own feeding grounds.

82
00:06:06,642 --> 00:06:07,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Eyes will pull you towards sights.

83
00:06:08,663 --> 00:06:10,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Nose will pull you towards smell.

84
00:06:10,604 --> 00:06:11,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Ears will pull you, and so on.

85
00:06:12,705 --> 00:06:14,026
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what your senses are.

86
00:06:14,166 --> 00:06:15,347
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what nature is.

87
00:06:16,067 --> 00:06:17,428
[SPEAKER_00]: So you actually go against that.

88
00:06:18,462 --> 00:06:20,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Against the stream, against the grain.

89
00:06:23,524 --> 00:06:28,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you going against the nature or are you understanding nature for what it is?

90
00:06:28,806 --> 00:06:30,567
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that means going against the nature.

91
00:06:31,528 --> 00:06:36,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, you know, there are different ways you can understand going against the nature.

92
00:06:36,270 --> 00:06:39,271
[SPEAKER_00]: People can just take that to mean denying the nature.

93
00:06:40,191 --> 00:06:44,052
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, in order to deny something, that thing has to be accepted first.

94
00:06:44,572 --> 00:06:48,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means denial of rebellion against nature is just confirmation of the nature.

95
00:06:49,593 --> 00:06:53,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Full understanding, on the other hand, is truly going against it.

96
00:06:53,714 --> 00:06:54,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Like overcoming it.

97
00:06:55,594 --> 00:06:56,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Swimming against the stream.

98
00:07:00,075 --> 00:07:00,816
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, sure.

99
00:07:02,336 --> 00:07:07,257
[SPEAKER_00]: Going against needs to be clarified, like individually, and understood correctly.

100
00:07:08,659 --> 00:07:10,516
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there's everything else that pertains to dhamma.

101
00:07:44,873 --> 00:07:56,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't there is a... once a person has entered the stream, sotāpanna, stream entry, right?

102
00:07:56,698 --> 00:07:58,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

103
00:08:00,379 --> 00:08:07,662
[SPEAKER_01]: Once someone has achieved sotāpanna, that's a guarantee that he wouldn't develop, that he wouldn't fall back to ignorance.

104
00:08:12,163 --> 00:08:13,603
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do you mean exactly by that?

105
00:08:13,843 --> 00:08:14,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Fall back.

106
00:08:14,584 --> 00:08:15,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Define fall back.

107
00:08:15,824 --> 00:08:17,544
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, he would...

108
00:08:17,564 --> 00:08:19,765
[SPEAKER_00]: He can't un-become, a sort of one.

109
00:08:19,805 --> 00:08:21,305
[SPEAKER_00]: He can't become putrijan again.

110
00:08:21,645 --> 00:08:25,586
[SPEAKER_00]: He can't have the personality of you and me.

111
00:08:26,247 --> 00:08:26,687
[SPEAKER_01]: He can't.

112
00:08:26,967 --> 00:08:28,007
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

113
00:08:28,847 --> 00:08:35,529
[SPEAKER_00]: But anything below that... Well, he can break all the precepts, despite the popular belief.

114
00:08:36,409 --> 00:08:38,590
[SPEAKER_00]: He can, you know, break all the precepts.

115
00:08:39,030 --> 00:08:40,310
[SPEAKER_00]: He cannot practice at all.

116
00:08:42,270 --> 00:09:03,134
[SPEAKER_00]: It will require a lot of carelessness, lots of exposure to unwholesome circumstances for him to grow that bad, because he inherently possesses a criteria of wholesome, and has that permanently available view, right view, that he can effortlessly engage in.

117
00:09:03,795 --> 00:09:08,956
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's quite unlikely, but it's possible, as there were cases in the service of a drunken sotāpanna and so on.

118
00:09:11,006 --> 00:09:13,247
[SPEAKER_00]: We spoke about it before, I think, I spoke there with Tanya.

119
00:09:13,867 --> 00:09:24,930
[SPEAKER_00]: The suttas mention clearly things that Sotāpanna cannot do, which is hold anything, any determination as permanent, any determination as pleasurable, anything presumed to be his self.

120
00:09:25,650 --> 00:09:40,234
[SPEAKER_00]: He can't kill his mother, can't kill his father, can't kill an arahant, can't violate the bhikkhuni, can't disparage the Buddha or wound the Buddha, can't take anybody else other than the Buddha as his teacher.

121
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:45,902
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all changing.

122
00:09:46,102 --> 00:09:47,823
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't follow any other teaching.

123
00:09:48,023 --> 00:09:48,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

124
00:09:48,343 --> 00:09:50,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the dhamma is not just a view.

125
00:09:51,204 --> 00:09:55,586
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a recognition of the, like, a universal criteria.

126
00:09:56,506 --> 00:10:00,628
[SPEAKER_00]: That it seems, you know, recognized, discerned, in your experience as a whole.

127
00:10:00,648 --> 00:10:04,069
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, once you discern that, those things are fundamental.

128
00:10:04,089 --> 00:10:06,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Those things are universal.

129
00:10:07,131 --> 00:10:08,211
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's confirmed.

130
00:10:08,471 --> 00:10:10,132
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, that becomes the

131
00:10:12,466 --> 00:10:14,588
[SPEAKER_00]: The core criteria for everything else.

132
00:10:14,628 --> 00:10:23,157
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whenever you come across any other view, it's just going to be seen as either according to the universal principle, the right principle, or it doesn't.

133
00:10:24,719 --> 00:10:32,367
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you come across some other teaching, you will say, as a sotāpanna, you'll see, ah, this actually affords to the principles of the dhamma.

134
00:10:32,667 --> 00:10:34,009
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this teaching is dhamma.

135
00:10:35,795 --> 00:10:39,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you see, you know, it doesn't have fault with dhamma, for such and such reasons, that's not dhamma.

136
00:10:40,699 --> 00:10:43,762
[SPEAKER_00]: And by saying dhamma, it refers to that universal principle.

137
00:10:45,063 --> 00:10:52,610
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to call it Buddhism, because often, today, now, modern Buddhism, contemporary Buddhism, has nothing to do with the actual dhamma.

138
00:10:53,470 --> 00:10:59,556
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the views that have evolved, wrong views, wrong methods, wrong principles, wrong techniques.

139
00:10:59,936 --> 00:11:03,459
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a religion, product of society, ignorant society, and so on.

140
00:11:05,076 --> 00:11:08,399
[SPEAKER_00]: But the dhamma, as a universal principle, stands and always will stand.

141
00:11:09,760 --> 00:11:13,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why liberation always remains possible.

142
00:11:15,724 --> 00:11:18,165
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha says, like rediscovering an ancient path.

143
00:11:19,306 --> 00:11:20,247
[SPEAKER_00]: He found the dhamma.

144
00:11:20,987 --> 00:11:23,289
[SPEAKER_00]: Like that has been worked before by others.

145
00:11:23,309 --> 00:11:24,930
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't get, ah, this...

146
00:11:24,990 --> 00:11:27,652
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't create anything, nor invent.

147
00:11:28,753 --> 00:11:31,995
[SPEAKER_00]: He rediscovered and then reformulated.

148
00:11:36,798 --> 00:11:51,628
[SPEAKER_01]: So, anyone that develops wisdom, but haven't attained a state, could develop it to a level that they'll fall back to saṃsāra continuously.

149
00:11:52,869 --> 00:11:55,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, what do you mean by state?

150
00:11:55,471 --> 00:11:56,571
[SPEAKER_01]: As in sotāpanna?

151
00:11:56,931 --> 00:11:57,652
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

152
00:11:57,932 --> 00:12:05,137
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, pratyūjana's wisdom, unless he develops it enough to cease to be a pratyūjana, he remains at risk.

153
00:12:17,505 --> 00:12:30,647
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, as the Aniruddha pointed out, a person, really, a genuine person, genuinely interested in practice, you know, you really have to start thinking about, you know, like, developing right view as not an optional thing.

154
00:12:31,747 --> 00:12:33,748
[SPEAKER_00]: It's your duty to become a sotāpanna.

155
00:12:35,348 --> 00:12:38,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, all of your faculties, reasoning, powers of reflection are intact.

156
00:12:39,149 --> 00:12:46,250
[SPEAKER_00]: You have no reason to not become a sotāpanna, except laziness and lack of effort, lack of commitment, lack of determination, and so on.

157
00:12:46,310 --> 00:12:47,150
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely no reason.

158
00:12:48,862 --> 00:12:54,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Family, children, wives, husbands, none of that is actual excuse.

159
00:12:55,507 --> 00:13:03,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, people make an excuse out of that, so they won't feel guilty on account of not following the way out.

160
00:13:04,772 --> 00:13:12,878
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they absolve themselves from responsibility for the practice, for determination, for giving up, because they say, oh, well, I have a family, I have duties and stuff.

161
00:13:13,339 --> 00:13:17,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's a worldly duty, but this pertains to something much more.

162
00:13:17,782 --> 00:13:23,446
[SPEAKER_00]: So, actually, the worldly duty can never serve as a reason or as an excuse.

163
00:13:24,047 --> 00:13:29,831
[SPEAKER_00]: It just might require more of your determination, but then there is no excuse for not having more determination.

164
00:13:30,603 --> 00:13:38,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless your faculties are impaired, you're mad, deranged or whatever else, then there's absolutely no excuse for not doing it.

165
00:13:43,398 --> 00:13:48,083
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you stop and think about it, how many times have you been reborn?

166
00:13:48,203 --> 00:13:50,866
[SPEAKER_00]: You might not know, but you can have a safe guess.

167
00:13:52,025 --> 00:14:08,677
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you have faith in the Buddha and trust in his words, he said how many times, countless times, eons and eons and eons and eons of rebirth in every possible realm that there was, there's nothing left unexperienced, unexplored, or anything like that.

168
00:14:08,697 --> 00:14:16,202
[SPEAKER_00]: So you realize, well, from that point of view, and that's why the Buddha would talk about that, is from that point of view, you can actually

169
00:14:18,903 --> 00:14:23,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Look at this entire life differently and realize, well, what is one life dedicated to dhamma?

170
00:14:24,327 --> 00:14:24,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing.

171
00:14:25,848 --> 00:14:32,952
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, now sometimes you feel, ah, well, I can't, like, give everything up in this life and just go and practice, because it just feels too much.

172
00:14:33,793 --> 00:14:36,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, from the perspective of only this life, it feels too much.

173
00:14:37,595 --> 00:14:42,358
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you start reflecting, like, from a greater point of view, you realize, well, what is one life?

174
00:14:43,539 --> 00:14:46,841
[SPEAKER_00]: Dedicating one life, succeeding, maybe failing,

175
00:14:48,435 --> 00:14:49,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe partially succeeding.

176
00:14:50,496 --> 00:14:51,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, at least you know you tried.

177
00:14:54,997 --> 00:15:04,400
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, like, you know, you can believe in rebirth or not, or whatever else, but the point is you don't know those things.

178
00:15:04,460 --> 00:15:06,261
[SPEAKER_00]: One thing you know for sure is you will die.

179
00:15:07,581 --> 00:15:14,564
[SPEAKER_00]: And what if the Buddha was right when he was talking about rebirth and everything else?

180
00:15:15,704 --> 00:15:17,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you willing to kind of

181
00:15:18,560 --> 00:15:32,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Just bet on it, and gamble on it, and spend this life carelessly, doing whatever you want, whatever pleases you, and then the Buddha turns out to be right, and then you end up much worse, for like thousands of years.

182
00:15:33,825 --> 00:15:43,169
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, the chances of coming back up to human realm, once you fall lower into animal realm, for example, was like the blind sea turtle that comes out every 500 years,

183
00:15:45,591 --> 00:15:49,475
[SPEAKER_00]: It just comes out to the top of the sea and goes down, so it doesn't linger.

184
00:15:50,556 --> 00:15:58,104
[SPEAKER_00]: That turtle has... and then there is a yoke with a piece of wood with a hole in it, being thrown around by the, you know, great winds from the sea, left and right, everywhere.

185
00:15:58,705 --> 00:16:02,168
[SPEAKER_00]: This turtle has a great chance of sticking its neck through that little hole.

186
00:16:03,390 --> 00:16:08,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Then somebody who has fallen back onto the animal realm has a chance of coming back to human realm.

187
00:16:09,188 --> 00:16:13,811
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you consider that, and like, again, you can't have an empirical proof.

188
00:16:13,851 --> 00:16:17,233
[SPEAKER_00]: Give me a scientific, well, it's because science is all based on senses.

189
00:16:17,793 --> 00:16:24,717
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're expecting to experience through your senses, as a proof, that which precedes your senses, rebirths and realms.

190
00:16:26,833 --> 00:16:39,757
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you just allow it as a valid possibility, you should then, out of... like, it will become obvious that the safe course of action would be, well, let me assume that there is, just in case that there is.

191
00:16:40,577 --> 00:16:41,838
[SPEAKER_00]: Because then, I'll be safe.

192
00:16:42,838 --> 00:16:50,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Rather than gambling and just deciding that there isn't... Because there is no more proof that there is no rebirth, than there is a proof that there is rebirth.

193
00:16:51,461 --> 00:16:53,241
[SPEAKER_00]: So, people say, well, I don't believe in rebirth.

194
00:16:53,261 --> 00:16:55,602
[SPEAKER_00]: They're no more justified than those who believe in it.

195
00:16:57,650 --> 00:17:02,812
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to accept both and then recognize, well, I don't really know whether there is or there isn't.

196
00:17:03,192 --> 00:17:11,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, I should act just in case that there is, because if there is, I'll be much worse off if I behave as if there isn't.

197
00:17:14,317 --> 00:17:21,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Wouldn't that be... wouldn't that same kind of argument be applied to a lot of different views?

198
00:17:22,140 --> 00:17:23,220
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you mean?

199
00:17:26,335 --> 00:17:40,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Just anything like, you know, whether it's a view about omnipresence or any sort of other philosophical or religious view.

200
00:17:42,123 --> 00:17:44,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, this is not really a philosophical view.

201
00:17:44,424 --> 00:17:49,927
[SPEAKER_00]: This clearly pertains something that precedes or comes after, succeeds after your existence that you know.

202
00:17:50,467 --> 00:17:53,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, it's truly metaphysical.

203
00:17:54,009 --> 00:17:55,090
[SPEAKER_00]: It can't be experienced.

204
00:17:56,767 --> 00:17:59,169
[SPEAKER_00]: It can be speculated about, and that's what you recognize.

205
00:17:59,629 --> 00:18:04,433
[SPEAKER_00]: This is inherently never, can never go beyond the level of speculation.

206
00:18:04,913 --> 00:18:07,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, I should act just in case.

207
00:18:08,195 --> 00:18:09,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Choose a wise course of action.

208
00:18:10,637 --> 00:18:15,100
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you know, views whether there is suffering and there is freedom of suffering, that's not metaphysical.

209
00:18:15,741 --> 00:18:23,847
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not like, you know, that can be speculative, but it can also be proven to yourself, fully understood, discerned, and confirmed.

210
00:18:27,698 --> 00:18:30,259
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is that, as per Ślavarīpa, that I'm metaphysical?

211
00:18:30,539 --> 00:18:31,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

212
00:18:31,940 --> 00:18:34,261
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, that's what the Buddha said, when he's at Vāyūna.

213
00:18:34,301 --> 00:18:35,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Beyond experiment.

214
00:18:35,222 --> 00:18:35,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

215
00:18:35,522 --> 00:18:37,423
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know, thus you should act.

216
00:18:37,943 --> 00:18:40,744
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, you don't even have to believe in it.

217
00:18:40,844 --> 00:18:45,927
[SPEAKER_00]: You can just use your reasoning right here and now, that you do have, and just discern.

218
00:18:46,487 --> 00:18:49,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, in case there is a rebirth, it's actually better to behave here.

219
00:18:50,609 --> 00:18:57,032
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, plus, if I do behave here, people will like me here, respect me here, find me trustworthy here, because I'm a moral person

220
00:18:57,834 --> 00:19:00,475
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm a virtuous person and they know they can rely on me.

221
00:19:01,776 --> 00:19:07,078
[SPEAKER_00]: And then if there is a next life, great, then I win, like on both accounts.

222
00:19:07,278 --> 00:19:09,019
[SPEAKER_00]: I win here, I win in the next life.

223
00:19:09,460 --> 00:19:11,300
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is a next life, well, so be it.

224
00:19:11,520 --> 00:19:12,601
[SPEAKER_00]: Then we all end up the same.

225
00:19:12,921 --> 00:19:22,666
[SPEAKER_00]: But at least here I live happily and respected by others and have my life smoother than if I'm a crook or criminal and always worrying being poor or whatever else.

226
00:19:22,726 --> 00:19:25,147
[SPEAKER_00]: So you really, you don't need a proof for it.

227
00:19:25,738 --> 00:19:29,621
[SPEAKER_00]: Just use your own reasoning and recognize that it's better to act as if there is.

228
00:19:31,703 --> 00:19:34,045
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all that matters, how you act right here, right now.

229
00:19:34,765 --> 00:19:36,467
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter whether there is or there isn't.

230
00:19:37,447 --> 00:19:48,076
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why I brought this up, because if you start thinking like that, you realize, what is dedicating this life, then, to the practice of dhamma?

231
00:19:48,156 --> 00:19:48,616
[SPEAKER_00]: It's nothing.

232
00:19:48,977 --> 00:19:49,677
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a small life.

233
00:19:51,559 --> 00:19:51,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Big deal.

234
00:19:52,319 --> 00:19:54,121
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be dead anyway, and you know that for sure.

235
00:19:55,542 --> 00:19:56,563
[SPEAKER_00]: And not in 50 years.

236
00:19:56,623 --> 00:19:58,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Might be much sooner, might be much later.

237
00:19:59,204 --> 00:20:01,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Point is, it will... it will happen.

238
00:20:02,225 --> 00:20:03,206
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it will come to an end.

239
00:20:03,906 --> 00:20:24,215
[SPEAKER_00]: So, having your... spending your time pursuing mundane goals, of course, just... just plain gratification of your senses, just scratching your itch, you know that, even from the worldly point of view, that it doesn't amount to anything more than that.

240
00:20:24,909 --> 00:20:25,469
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you're dead.

241
00:20:26,630 --> 00:20:28,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, your senses start breaking up.

242
00:20:29,793 --> 00:20:31,254
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't enjoy them anymore.

243
00:20:31,714 --> 00:20:33,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything becomes rather unpleasant.

244
00:20:34,736 --> 00:20:35,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Your hearing goes.

245
00:20:35,857 --> 00:20:37,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Your body doesn't listen.

246
00:20:37,278 --> 00:20:38,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Sickness sets in.

247
00:20:38,639 --> 00:20:39,240
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you're dead.

248
00:20:40,241 --> 00:20:41,461
[SPEAKER_00]: And how you spend the life?

249
00:20:43,523 --> 00:20:49,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Being completely controlled by those five wild animals, who just yanking you left and right, as they please.

250
00:20:50,447 --> 00:20:54,269
[SPEAKER_00]: And now you're kind of stuck all too weak to do anything better, and then you die.

251
00:20:55,070 --> 00:20:58,391
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, if there is the next life, then you'll be really, really screwed.

252
00:21:03,214 --> 00:21:11,779
[SPEAKER_02]: It is, obviously, what I've heard common, you know, just enjoy the life now.

253
00:21:12,159 --> 00:21:14,220
[SPEAKER_02]: Just only what you can... wipe only what you have.

254
00:21:16,001 --> 00:21:17,242
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, just enjoy it.

255
00:21:18,422 --> 00:21:19,363
[SPEAKER_02]: Do the best you can.

256
00:21:29,602 --> 00:21:33,204
[SPEAKER_00]: But as I said, you don't even have to wait for the next life or so.

257
00:21:33,224 --> 00:21:42,048
[SPEAKER_00]: You can see that that that view and logic, well, works only while you have your health, you have your youth, you have your life.

258
00:21:42,589 --> 00:21:46,551
[SPEAKER_00]: And none of those things are guaranteed that you're going to have the very next moment.

259
00:21:48,422 --> 00:21:55,065
[SPEAKER_00]: All of them are based upon a gratuitous assumption that you'll live forever, or at least for a long time, and that you will always be healthy.

260
00:21:55,305 --> 00:22:00,788
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah, all... yeah, life is... life is happening so that you can go out and enjoy it.

261
00:22:00,968 --> 00:22:01,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

262
00:22:01,348 --> 00:22:05,870
[SPEAKER_02]: But what about the person who's, you know, born with no legs or paralyzed?

263
00:22:05,890 --> 00:22:08,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Or what about you just next day you find you have cancer?

264
00:22:08,251 --> 00:22:08,751
[SPEAKER_02]: Does it work?

265
00:22:08,771 --> 00:22:09,351
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't apply.

266
00:22:09,391 --> 00:22:10,212
[SPEAKER_02]: Tell me now.

267
00:22:10,232 --> 00:22:11,212
[SPEAKER_00]: How are you going to enjoy that?

268
00:22:12,873 --> 00:22:17,515
[SPEAKER_00]: What happens to their view then, when you realize, oh, I'm subject to death?

269
00:22:19,257 --> 00:22:20,498
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, it's confirmed.

270
00:22:22,460 --> 00:22:22,980
[SPEAKER_02]: Those are there.

271
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:24,101
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a fact, that's for sure.

272
00:22:26,504 --> 00:22:27,264
[SPEAKER_02]: You're going to die.

273
00:22:27,725 --> 00:22:29,346
[SPEAKER_02]: What's going to happen afterwards is not...

274
00:22:30,927 --> 00:22:31,768
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what's the point?

275
00:22:35,391 --> 00:22:37,373
[SPEAKER_02]: What's the point of life, you know, if you just say?

276
00:22:39,915 --> 00:22:42,498
[SPEAKER_02]: If you have the view that this is all there is,

277
00:22:45,798 --> 00:22:50,220
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, people who say that don't think very far, further than that, really.

278
00:22:51,721 --> 00:22:56,784
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, because if you think further than that, what... then what's the point of anything?

279
00:22:57,504 --> 00:23:11,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it really the point just to be enjoying... Again, as I just said, like, a few minutes ago, saying, like, those people having a view, this is all there is, well, that's something you decided as a view.

280
00:23:12,091 --> 00:23:13,372
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know that for a fact.

281
00:23:14,621 --> 00:23:18,943
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're no more justified in deciding that, than deciding, no, this isn't what it is.

282
00:23:19,623 --> 00:23:21,464
[SPEAKER_00]: And as it turns out, you're less justified.

283
00:23:21,504 --> 00:23:21,764
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

284
00:23:21,804 --> 00:23:27,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's much less... much riskier course of assumption.

285
00:23:27,887 --> 00:23:28,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is all there is.

286
00:23:28,828 --> 00:23:30,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Do what you can, while you can.

287
00:23:31,949 --> 00:23:35,171
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you realize, well, what if this isn't all there is?

288
00:23:35,211 --> 00:23:43,435
[SPEAKER_00]: And all my time that I had, I spent misbehaving and just running after like an animal, after any gratification I could get.

289
00:23:46,076 --> 00:23:47,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you end up much worse.

290
00:23:47,597 --> 00:23:51,438
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to consider it that you don't know whether this is all there is.

291
00:23:51,498 --> 00:23:54,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, it's better to act as if it isn't all there is.

292
00:23:55,959 --> 00:23:56,179
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

293
00:23:56,239 --> 00:24:04,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, if there is something afterwards, those people who do say that there is something afterwards, they say, because of that, you should behave right here, right now.

294
00:24:06,403 --> 00:24:08,463
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is a good sense in that.

295
00:24:08,643 --> 00:24:12,264
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can see that for yourself, right here, right now, even as a Putrajana.

296
00:24:12,484 --> 00:24:14,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Putrajanas can see that as well.

297
00:24:15,978 --> 00:24:26,704
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, obviously, if you want even more, if you want to be free from suffering right here, right now, let alone any future instances, then, yeah, you have to apply yourself even harder.

298
00:24:26,724 --> 00:24:29,505
[SPEAKER_00]: But, again, it comes from the from the same perspective.

299
00:24:30,586 --> 00:24:37,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Accepting the fact, well, rather recognizing the fact that one thing is sure is you will grow sick, age and die.

300
00:24:38,710 --> 00:24:39,091
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

301
00:24:40,191 --> 00:24:43,313
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know, spend your life enjoying your senses.

302
00:24:44,333 --> 00:24:45,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Works when you're 20 years old.

303
00:24:45,996 --> 00:24:47,197
[SPEAKER_00]: When you think you'll live forever...

304
00:24:48,857 --> 00:24:53,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind you, you can be 50 years old and still say that, because you think you'll live forever, and you're healthy at the time.

305
00:24:54,540 --> 00:25:00,163
[SPEAKER_00]: But, finally, a sick person, in a hospital, bedridden, saying that, holding that view.

306
00:25:02,324 --> 00:25:03,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, enjoy your life.

307
00:25:04,445 --> 00:25:05,026
[SPEAKER_00]: This is great.

308
00:25:05,086 --> 00:25:12,950
[SPEAKER_00]: See, I'm still alive, bedridden in a hospital, oozing out whatever else, I don't know, depending on the illness, I guess.

309
00:25:14,500 --> 00:25:18,783
[SPEAKER_00]: And that person, would they be saying, oh, yeah, enjoy your senses while you're still alive?

310
00:25:19,844 --> 00:25:21,325
[SPEAKER_02]: They might be telling others that.

311
00:25:21,845 --> 00:25:22,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they might.

312
00:25:22,566 --> 00:25:23,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, see, now I'm sick.

313
00:25:24,327 --> 00:25:25,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Really make the most of it.

314
00:25:25,828 --> 00:25:26,168
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

315
00:25:26,809 --> 00:25:28,810
[SPEAKER_00]: And they might listen because the others are not sick.

316
00:25:29,731 --> 00:25:31,112
[SPEAKER_00]: They're intoxicated with health.

317
00:25:32,113 --> 00:25:33,213
[SPEAKER_00]: And you know that for yourself.

318
00:25:33,294 --> 00:25:35,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody experienced that, more or less.

319
00:25:36,776 --> 00:25:38,077
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're healthy, you don't care.

320
00:25:38,597 --> 00:25:39,318
[SPEAKER_00]: When you get sick,

321
00:25:40,151 --> 00:25:49,859
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you get all really worried, and then it's all... and you got sick because of your stupidity or foolishness, and then you make all those grand... you think, oh, I'll never do that again, you know.

322
00:25:50,220 --> 00:25:54,023
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you recover, and then you're back to the old ways you completely forgot.

323
00:25:54,403 --> 00:25:57,065
[SPEAKER_00]: It's pure intoxication with what you have at the time.

324
00:25:58,387 --> 00:26:01,409
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like complete lack of mindfulness, absence.

325
00:26:05,272 --> 00:26:08,175
[SPEAKER_00]: And same, you make grand plans and projects and

326
00:26:09,429 --> 00:26:16,735
[SPEAKER_00]: And ideas and hopes and whatever else, just because you assume you will live forever, you assume your life will last for a long time.

327
00:26:19,036 --> 00:26:26,562
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you stop and think about that assumption, it's completely gratuitous, as every assumption is, but this one, it really can hit you.

328
00:26:41,677 --> 00:27:07,316
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as the Buddha said, even if somebody went forth and they don't achieve anything, but they don't disrobe, they stay, they keep the rules and they keep trying to practice, even if they don't get anywhere, even if they spend every single day for the rest of their life in tears, in pain, imposed by the restraint, it's still better than going back.

329
00:27:08,846 --> 00:27:09,026
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

330
00:27:09,086 --> 00:27:23,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you've been around so long, so many times, that spending a third of your lifetime now, crying in pain, but holding that restraint, will actually contribute much more than just giving in to the senses.

331
00:27:25,514 --> 00:27:32,197
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, at the same time, you start to think, well, it's impossible to spend every single day for the rest of your life in tears and pain.

332
00:27:33,137 --> 00:27:35,298
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter how great that pain is, it will fade.

333
00:27:36,990 --> 00:27:41,951
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're ready for every single day of pain, anything less than that would be a blessing.

334
00:28:07,583 --> 00:28:14,447
[SPEAKER_00]: And people, nobody likes restraint, because it feels like a chore, feels like an effort.

335
00:28:18,008 --> 00:28:24,132
[SPEAKER_00]: And it feels like that only from the point of view where your sensuality feels effortless.

336
00:28:26,553 --> 00:28:34,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, the actual gratification of the senses might feel effortless, is effortless, but it never comes on its own.

337
00:28:35,037 --> 00:28:36,278
[SPEAKER_00]: It always comes with a price.

338
00:28:38,570 --> 00:28:44,973
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not seeing the connection between sensuality and gratification and the price and toll it takes.

339
00:28:46,753 --> 00:29:05,561
[SPEAKER_00]: You reflect on the connection long enough, and it will become apparent because it is there, and then you realize it's much harder, much more taxing, much more... much more of a chore to be following your senses and enjoying them than saying no to the whole thing.

340
00:29:08,132 --> 00:29:12,994
[SPEAKER_00]: But that connection needs to be dwelt on long enough until it becomes apparent, because it's not apparent.

341
00:29:13,034 --> 00:29:16,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it were apparent so easily, everybody would be free from sensuality.

342
00:29:19,977 --> 00:29:36,463
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can start with a simple reflection of, if you're a layperson, living a household life, enjoying the pleasures of your senses, means pleasant tastes, touches, sounds,

343
00:29:37,849 --> 00:29:42,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Finding a pleasant wife or a girlfriend, living a pleasant life.

344
00:29:44,515 --> 00:29:45,395
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you achieve that?

345
00:29:47,097 --> 00:29:47,697
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to work.

346
00:29:49,799 --> 00:29:50,540
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to earn.

347
00:29:51,921 --> 00:29:57,365
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to really put up with stuff you don't like, very often.

348
00:29:57,806 --> 00:30:02,590
[SPEAKER_00]: You might find a work you like, you might be your own boss, but then you have to protect it.

349
00:30:03,190 --> 00:30:05,412
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to fend off people who are the same.

350
00:30:06,405 --> 00:30:10,386
[SPEAKER_00]: People who cheat on you, people who lie on you, people who want to take things away from you.

351
00:30:10,906 --> 00:30:11,886
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's always that risk.

352
00:30:13,347 --> 00:30:15,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you're committing your life to it.

353
00:30:16,347 --> 00:30:24,029
[SPEAKER_00]: You're spending eighty... ninety percent of doing things you don't want, just so you get that five percent enjoyment.

354
00:30:24,949 --> 00:30:28,370
[SPEAKER_00]: It... it doesn't... it doesn't... doesn't feel directly related to it.

355
00:30:29,270 --> 00:30:31,311
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the ignorance obscuring it.

356
00:30:32,191 --> 00:30:35,612
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you realize that it's impossible to have enjoyment,

357
00:30:36,881 --> 00:30:43,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Without 95% of pain revolving around it, directly or indirectly, you realize that enjoyment is not worth it.

358
00:30:46,145 --> 00:30:51,848
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when the when the Buddha talks about seeing the danger in the sensuality.

359
00:30:53,328 --> 00:31:06,674
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by choosing to pursue the ways of the senses, which is basically choosing to have family, children, wives or husbands, whatever else, living that kind of life,

360
00:31:07,593 --> 00:31:15,257
[SPEAKER_00]: You accept that whole sensual domain where these things pertain, where these things are.

361
00:31:15,637 --> 00:31:20,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you don't practice restraint, but you practice pursuit.

362
00:31:21,260 --> 00:31:22,441
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a form of commitment.

363
00:31:22,761 --> 00:31:32,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Pursuing your senses is a form of commitment, but then you realize by accepting, by engaging in that pursuit, you make yourself liable to a great loss.

364
00:31:34,090 --> 00:31:40,052
[SPEAKER_00]: So, say, you commit yourself to a pursuit of having a family, having children.

365
00:31:41,233 --> 00:31:48,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, now that you have family and children, you have to work harder for them, for the food, for the clothing, for the school.

366
00:31:48,976 --> 00:31:49,796
[SPEAKER_00]: What if they get sick?

367
00:31:51,096 --> 00:31:51,997
[SPEAKER_00]: What if they die?

368
00:31:53,057 --> 00:32:01,220
[SPEAKER_00]: You spend twenty, thirty years cultivating, investing in those things, dedicating your whole life praising your children.

369
00:32:02,661 --> 00:32:03,681
[SPEAKER_00]: Then they turn away on you.

370
00:32:04,620 --> 00:32:10,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Or they die, or an accident happened, and it just snatched away in a second.

371
00:32:10,766 --> 00:32:14,088
[SPEAKER_00]: And you have not had say in that from the beginning.

372
00:32:15,250 --> 00:32:19,493
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, from the day one, you have no say in the destiny of your child.

373
00:32:20,054 --> 00:32:25,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you have no say in your own destiny, but you have ignored that when you decided to pursue the senses.

374
00:32:25,879 --> 00:32:29,042
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you reflect that, you realize that even the pursuit of the senses

375
00:32:31,603 --> 00:32:33,124
[SPEAKER_00]: It's actually completely out of your hands.

376
00:32:33,544 --> 00:32:36,345
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the suttas compare it to, like, climbing a tree.

377
00:32:37,005 --> 00:32:42,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, you see the ripe mangoes on the tree, so you climb the tree to eat them, to enjoy them.

378
00:32:43,707 --> 00:32:51,349
[SPEAKER_00]: And then somebody else, while you're on top of that tree, comes, and he can't climb the tree, so he decides to cut it.

379
00:32:51,369 --> 00:32:53,470
[SPEAKER_00]: While you're still on the top, he doesn't see or he doesn't care.

380
00:32:53,530 --> 00:32:56,351
[SPEAKER_00]: He chops the tree, you fall down, break in it, and you're done.

381
00:32:57,771 --> 00:32:59,252
[SPEAKER_00]: And pursue the senses,

382
00:33:00,814 --> 00:33:01,855
[SPEAKER_00]: Exposes you to that.

383
00:33:01,955 --> 00:33:11,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Means going down, or climbing up the tree, or for kind, or the other, and something might come at a direct cross-purpose, and just snatch it away from you.

384
00:33:12,360 --> 00:33:16,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Be it an accident, being other people, being both, who knows?

385
00:33:16,922 --> 00:33:19,303
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, the exposure is there.

386
00:33:20,584 --> 00:33:22,705
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is the inherent danger in that pursuit.

387
00:33:25,306 --> 00:33:27,487
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize that danger is 95% of all.

388
00:33:28,988 --> 00:33:29,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it worth

389
00:33:30,582 --> 00:33:40,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Risking your life, putting your head on a chopping block, when there is a good chance of axe falling down, just so you get that five percent thrill.

390
00:33:43,912 --> 00:33:49,576
[SPEAKER_00]: And it is worth, that gratification is worth, for as long as you don't establish that connection between the two.

391
00:33:49,596 --> 00:33:49,836
[SPEAKER_00]: i.e.

392
00:33:50,196 --> 00:33:52,638
[SPEAKER_00]: for as long as you don't see the danger in some thrill.

393
00:33:54,113 --> 00:34:01,736
[SPEAKER_00]: You establish the connection, and then the danger became apparent, the visible, the mind turned away, experience, dispassion, as the sūtras describe it.

394
00:34:02,936 --> 00:34:07,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, like, the disgust became, well, real.

395
00:34:08,218 --> 00:34:12,100
[SPEAKER_00]: This is danger, this is not what... mind turned away, never to go back to it.

396
00:34:12,640 --> 00:34:12,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

397
00:34:12,840 --> 00:34:13,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it is like that.

398
00:34:14,321 --> 00:34:17,962
[SPEAKER_00]: If sensuality and gratification would not inherently be dangerous,

399
00:34:19,117 --> 00:34:26,439
[SPEAKER_00]: And risky, and much, like, much more revolving around pain than dissatisfaction.

400
00:34:27,399 --> 00:34:31,400
[SPEAKER_00]: If it wouldn't be inherently that, then it wouldn't be unwholesome.

401
00:34:32,300 --> 00:34:34,481
[SPEAKER_00]: And that certainly freedom from it would not be possible.

402
00:34:34,701 --> 00:34:45,803
[SPEAKER_00]: But because it is inherently dangerous, risky, and not worthy, it is inherently like that, then, by dwelling on that, freedom from it is possible.

403
00:34:46,443 --> 00:34:48,304
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you realize, saying no,

404
00:34:49,242 --> 00:34:55,587
[SPEAKER_00]: As a form of restraint, the sensuality, it's 5% of effort, 95% of peace.

405
00:34:57,028 --> 00:34:58,029
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's well worth it.

406
00:34:58,749 --> 00:35:03,693
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when, when you find, when, when a person finds pleasure of renunciation.

407
00:35:04,574 --> 00:35:12,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, actually, you, you experience pleasure by being, by recognizing strength that you have of saying no.

408
00:35:13,100 --> 00:35:16,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Not being, you are stronger than senses, senses are not stronger than you.

409
00:35:21,387 --> 00:35:30,515
[SPEAKER_02]: There's also a view that you will actually, through those challenges in life, through having a family child, eh, you will get to know yourself better.

410
00:35:30,655 --> 00:35:30,855
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

411
00:35:31,155 --> 00:35:31,896
[SPEAKER_02]: You will evolve.

412
00:35:32,296 --> 00:35:39,042
[SPEAKER_00]: You will, you will evolve in a, in a way, in a, in a, in a, in a discovering different ways of pursuing the senses.

413
00:35:39,782 --> 00:35:48,970
[SPEAKER_00]: But whether you evolve this way or that way, bottom line is, it's still within the nature of pursuing the senses, and that's still within the nature of that danger and risk.

414
00:35:49,848 --> 00:35:54,591
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, any evolving that you do will be within that.

415
00:35:55,451 --> 00:36:03,496
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically like going in a muddy swamp and finding different ways to wash yourself with the muddy water.

416
00:36:04,677 --> 00:36:09,039
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter how elaborate your ways become, it's still filthy water.

417
00:36:09,339 --> 00:36:11,541
[SPEAKER_00]: It means you'll never be clean.

418
00:36:15,023 --> 00:36:16,484
[SPEAKER_02]: Evolve in the same way.

419
00:36:20,189 --> 00:36:25,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, you can be in prison, in prison, behind the locked door.

420
00:36:26,472 --> 00:36:29,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, you can involve in that environment.

421
00:36:29,694 --> 00:36:32,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever involving you do, it will be involving in the prison.

422
00:36:32,935 --> 00:36:34,636
[SPEAKER_00]: But you really want to be as part of the prison.

423
00:36:35,756 --> 00:36:41,299
[SPEAKER_00]: It's better to be uninvolved, out of the prison, than involved, in further comments, in the prison.

424
00:36:41,359 --> 00:36:44,481
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's not really involvement, it's just a further proliferation.

425
00:36:45,241 --> 00:36:47,242
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's people say that, they never say it

426
00:36:48,249 --> 00:36:55,034
[SPEAKER_00]: For any other sense, any other purpose than a purpose of justifying their pursuit of the senses upon which they already decided.

427
00:36:55,955 --> 00:37:00,238
[SPEAKER_00]: But now they need to keep justifying it.

428
00:37:00,398 --> 00:37:01,179
[SPEAKER_00]: And sure, you can.

429
00:37:01,579 --> 00:37:10,887
[SPEAKER_00]: But you do that to others because you might feel guilty or you might be... you might feel watched or judged.

430
00:37:12,047 --> 00:37:13,248
[SPEAKER_00]: So you justify yourself.

431
00:37:14,489 --> 00:37:15,150
[SPEAKER_00]: But basically,

432
00:37:16,528 --> 00:37:21,311
[SPEAKER_00]: That's done already when you're deeply down, committed to the senses.

433
00:37:21,331 --> 00:37:24,113
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now you're just trying to make the best out of it.

434
00:37:24,553 --> 00:37:29,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Cover up any doubt in regard to your choices, in regard to your commitment.

435
00:37:30,497 --> 00:37:33,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Above all, absorb all the responsibility for it.

436
00:37:33,780 --> 00:37:35,161
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, well, you know, I can't.

437
00:37:35,321 --> 00:37:38,463
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't go forth now because I have wife and children.

438
00:37:38,703 --> 00:37:44,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they were not given to you, like, you know, out of nowhere.

439
00:37:45,192 --> 00:37:46,012
[SPEAKER_00]: You've chosen wrong.

440
00:37:46,392 --> 00:37:52,994
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, what you're saying, you can't go forth, because of the choices you make, and the choices that you're still accepting.

441
00:37:53,974 --> 00:37:58,795
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't... and that means you can't go forth, because you're choosing to not go forth.

442
00:38:00,055 --> 00:38:00,395
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

443
00:38:01,316 --> 00:38:07,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, if you put it like that, you realize I'm fully responsible for not going forth, for not practicing the dhamma.

444
00:38:11,665 --> 00:38:23,470
[SPEAKER_00]: So, saying, oh, well, you evolve, you grow, by engaging in these challenges, it's like, well, you really grow by recognizing how to completely overcome those challenges so they don't arise.

445
00:38:23,630 --> 00:38:28,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would you keep yourself in the environment where those challenges will be coming your way?

446
00:38:29,572 --> 00:38:29,992
[SPEAKER_00]: It's silly.

447
00:38:30,932 --> 00:38:34,854
[SPEAKER_00]: Surely, the wisdom is finding a place that's free from any challenge.

448
00:38:39,148 --> 00:38:43,873
[SPEAKER_00]: But only a person who doesn't know how to free themselves from their challenges will say, no, no, no, challenges are good.

449
00:38:44,534 --> 00:38:47,277
[SPEAKER_00]: They only say that because they don't know how to escape them, how to overcome them.

450
00:38:48,939 --> 00:38:54,346
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they need to make themselves feel important or more valuable than they actually are.

451
00:39:06,026 --> 00:39:12,989
[SPEAKER_02]: I remember we spoke a while back about seeing an attractive thing.

452
00:39:14,550 --> 00:39:22,874
[SPEAKER_02]: I've seen... when you see an attractive object, like a car, I think, it feels painful by it.

453
00:39:23,374 --> 00:39:23,634
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

454
00:39:23,714 --> 00:39:31,598
[SPEAKER_02]: Say, if I see... if I see a beautiful woman walking around in the city, I do...

455
00:39:32,392 --> 00:39:38,257
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't dwell, I try not to dwell on this, but I know, I can see the beauty from the corner of my eye, can't I?

456
00:39:38,597 --> 00:39:41,639
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't have to look at it, I can see it on the other side of the road.

457
00:39:43,000 --> 00:39:46,243
[SPEAKER_02]: And then I have to restrain myself.

458
00:39:46,783 --> 00:39:56,430
[SPEAKER_02]: But I was reflecting a bit further, like, that sign of beauty comes with pain somehow.

459
00:39:58,045 --> 00:39:58,285
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

460
00:39:58,926 --> 00:39:59,886
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I was saying before.

461
00:40:00,247 --> 00:40:02,688
[SPEAKER_00]: That sign of beauty is that which is painful.

462
00:40:02,808 --> 00:40:04,850
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the restraint.

463
00:40:04,930 --> 00:40:13,636
[SPEAKER_00]: It's when you say no to a prospect of pleasure that is pulling you, whether it's a pleasurable sight, sound, smell, or any other sense.

464
00:40:14,397 --> 00:40:17,599
[SPEAKER_00]: It feels like the pain has arisen because you said no.

465
00:40:18,720 --> 00:40:19,640
[SPEAKER_00]: If only you go with it.

466
00:40:20,021 --> 00:40:20,161
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

467
00:40:20,361 --> 00:40:25,965
[SPEAKER_00]: The pain has arisen because you've been yanked by that animal of that sense.

468
00:40:27,256 --> 00:40:31,199
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just usually, you immediately go with it, so there is no yank.

469
00:40:31,439 --> 00:40:34,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, as the leash starts pulling you, you go with it.

470
00:40:35,563 --> 00:40:37,044
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is no tension created.

471
00:40:38,165 --> 00:40:41,327
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just feel smooth sailing, because you're just being driven.

472
00:40:44,230 --> 00:40:52,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, when you start reflecting on it, you sort of don't go instantaneously, and then there's a pain arising, and you think, oh, this pain has arisen,

473
00:40:53,705 --> 00:40:56,847
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I didn't go immediately with this animal that pulled me.

474
00:40:57,527 --> 00:40:58,448
[SPEAKER_00]: An animal of sight.

475
00:40:59,428 --> 00:41:00,229
[SPEAKER_00]: But actually, no.

476
00:41:00,589 --> 00:41:02,110
[SPEAKER_00]: You go a step further, and you realize, no.

477
00:41:02,570 --> 00:41:05,391
[SPEAKER_00]: The pain has arisen because the stupid animal yanked me.

478
00:41:07,072 --> 00:41:11,735
[SPEAKER_00]: And for as long as I go with it, it will keep yanking, because I'm allowing it to do it.

479
00:41:12,015 --> 00:41:17,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Same, you take a dog on a leash, you let it yank your arm, and that's what you get used to.

480
00:41:21,178 --> 00:41:29,240
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of going with it, you invest the effort to overpower that yank, so you actually control the animal.

481
00:41:29,740 --> 00:41:32,441
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, eventually, animal learns it's pointless to yank.

482
00:41:34,142 --> 00:41:35,682
[SPEAKER_00]: I won't be let go when I yank.

483
00:41:36,102 --> 00:41:36,822
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it listens.

484
00:41:37,522 --> 00:41:41,083
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it goes where you direct it to go.

485
00:41:41,644 --> 00:41:42,984
[SPEAKER_00]: But that needs to...

486
00:41:43,524 --> 00:41:46,665
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, the inevitable, oh, I've been yanked.

487
00:41:46,685 --> 00:41:50,546
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because I didn't go with the animal as quickly as I should have.

488
00:41:51,631 --> 00:41:54,073
[SPEAKER_00]: But no, you've been yanked because the animal pulled one.

489
00:41:55,394 --> 00:42:07,684
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to endure that initial pain and also see through it as not originating from you saying no to the animal that started to run with you holding onto its chain.

490
00:42:08,565 --> 00:42:11,607
[SPEAKER_00]: But it has a reason because the animal has started... the animal is wild.

491
00:42:11,687 --> 00:42:12,668
[SPEAKER_00]: The animal is untrained.

492
00:42:12,728 --> 00:42:13,629
[SPEAKER_00]: The animal yanks.

493
00:42:14,190 --> 00:42:15,590
[SPEAKER_00]: The eye goes towards that...

494
00:42:15,630 --> 00:42:16,131
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

495
00:42:16,151 --> 00:42:17,571
[SPEAKER_00]: The eye goes for the sights.

496
00:42:17,831 --> 00:42:18,291
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what...

497
00:42:18,331 --> 00:42:24,033
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when there is a pain of a pleasurable sight, it's because of the eye goes there, not because you're saying no to it.

498
00:42:28,114 --> 00:42:30,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, you stick to saying no to it.

499
00:42:31,175 --> 00:42:35,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Until, eventually, you overpower the eye, and it stops linking you.

500
00:42:36,577 --> 00:42:37,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it realizes it's pointless.

501
00:42:38,418 --> 00:42:39,498
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you tie the dog,

502
00:42:40,410 --> 00:42:43,653
[SPEAKER_00]: To a post, it will yank and yank, and then... No.

503
00:42:43,853 --> 00:42:45,594
[SPEAKER_00]: The post doesn't budge, doesn't break.

504
00:42:45,894 --> 00:42:47,335
[SPEAKER_00]: It will sit down and calm down.

505
00:42:47,716 --> 00:42:49,998
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, that's the sutta simile.

506
00:42:50,038 --> 00:42:53,440
[SPEAKER_00]: When you... like a strong post, you tie these five animals to a chain.

507
00:42:53,460 --> 00:42:56,243
[SPEAKER_00]: They will go wild and try to pull the rope.

508
00:42:57,283 --> 00:43:00,005
[SPEAKER_00]: When they can't break it, eventually, they'll quiet down and sit down.

509
00:43:00,486 --> 00:43:03,088
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you can take them off for a walk, and they'll be obedient.

510
00:43:07,551 --> 00:43:09,373
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the training of the senses.

511
00:43:11,035 --> 00:43:23,945
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, with the eye, you're going into the city, where it lights pretty, pretty girls, beautiful women.

512
00:43:25,086 --> 00:43:38,216
[SPEAKER_02]: And, like I just said now, you see that it, you can see that it's not yours, because you realize that there's, all of a sudden, you're looking at this pretty girl on the other side of the road,

513
00:43:39,122 --> 00:43:56,447
[SPEAKER_02]: But the whole time, your attention, your intention was, say, you're looking at the pavement, following you, or whatever, and then, all of a sudden, my whole, you know, my head turns, my vision turns to that specific thing, which I can say, yeah, that's attractive.

514
00:43:56,587 --> 00:43:56,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

515
00:43:57,027 --> 00:44:00,088
[SPEAKER_02]: From the hundreds of people walking around... Who are not attractive.

516
00:44:00,108 --> 00:44:00,708
[SPEAKER_02]: Who are not attractive.

517
00:44:00,808 --> 00:44:03,809
[SPEAKER_02]: I picked the one, which I'm not even looking at, who is attractive.

518
00:44:04,109 --> 00:44:05,129
[SPEAKER_02]: It's because my eye

519
00:44:06,232 --> 00:44:07,753
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not mine.

520
00:44:08,073 --> 00:44:09,633
[SPEAKER_02]: It's seen more than...

521
00:44:09,673 --> 00:44:10,574
[SPEAKER_00]: The eye wants that.

522
00:44:10,654 --> 00:44:11,594
[SPEAKER_00]: The eye sees that.

523
00:44:11,894 --> 00:44:17,937
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's the same, like, the same that Sutta was talking about yesterday, when Buddha asked the man, what do you think?

524
00:44:18,177 --> 00:44:19,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is more numerous?

525
00:44:19,277 --> 00:44:21,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that you haven't seen or things that you've seen?

526
00:44:22,098 --> 00:44:23,319
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that you haven't seen, obviously.

527
00:44:24,379 --> 00:44:28,080
[SPEAKER_00]: You use... because of that, you abandon all the things that you've seen.

528
00:44:28,841 --> 00:44:32,662
[SPEAKER_00]: All the things that you will see will always be less numerous than things that are unseen.

529
00:44:33,803 --> 00:44:34,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus,

530
00:44:35,096 --> 00:44:40,260
[SPEAKER_00]: But reflecting on that unseen, you can abandon everything that you've seen, and you will see.

531
00:44:40,721 --> 00:44:42,482
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, which is more numerous?

532
00:44:43,062 --> 00:44:43,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Beautiful or ugly?

533
00:44:45,444 --> 00:44:45,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Ugly.

534
00:44:47,026 --> 00:44:49,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Out of a hundred people, you might find one or five attractive.

535
00:44:50,969 --> 00:44:57,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, they are of the same material, of the same make, like 95 unattractive ones.

536
00:44:57,674 --> 00:45:02,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Old, sick, aging, overweight, whatever.

537
00:45:03,007 --> 00:45:07,910
[SPEAKER_00]: Things you don't find attractive, they're more numerous than the ones you pick as attractive.

538
00:45:09,151 --> 00:45:13,654
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize how circumstantial attractiveness is, and how random, and how exactly.

539
00:45:14,135 --> 00:45:16,156
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing to do with you.

540
00:45:16,176 --> 00:45:19,959
[SPEAKER_00]: It's entirely your eye finding this circumstantially pleasing.

541
00:45:20,819 --> 00:45:22,600
[SPEAKER_00]: But you just run with it.

542
00:45:22,741 --> 00:45:22,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

543
00:45:23,041 --> 00:45:23,901
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it pulls you.

544
